positive roots and eight negative, or all roots are real and none are positive. But given the first coefficient is positive, then the next term is -20, then +135. So the first term is positive, then a large negative, then a positive. Hence, the function starts at +∞, decreases to a minimum, then increases. Given that there are two sign changes, there are two positive real roots and possibly other roots. Since all roots are real, the remaining roots must be negative. But the sum is 20. Hence, it\'s possible.\n\nBut according to Descartes\' Rule, the possible number of positive roots is 2 or 0. Hence, two positive roots and eight negative roots, or all roots negative. But if all roots were negative, their sum would be negative, which contradicts the sum of 20. Therefore, the only possibility is two positive roots and eight negative roots. Hence, the roots come in two positive values and eight negative values. But maybe two distinct positive roots and eight of the other, or all roots are duplicated.\n\nBut in the problem statement, it\'s not specified whether the roots are distinct. So the roots could be two different positive numbers and six other roots and two duplicates.\n\nAlternatively, the roots could be arranged as two distinct positive roots and eight negative roots. But we need to find the largest real number that occurs as a root.\n\nBut how to compute this? Let me suppose that two roots are a and b (positive) and the other eight roots are c (negative). Then:\n\nSum: a + b +8c=20.\n\nSum of products two at a time:\n\nab + a*8c +b*8c +64c2=ab +8c(a +b) +64c2=135.\n\nBut this seems complex. Alternatively, maybe the two positive roots are equal, to simplify.\n\nLet a =b. Then:\n\nSum: 2a +8c=20→a +4c=10.\n\nSum of products two at a time:\n\na2 +8c(2a) +64c2= a2 +16a c +64c2=135.\n\nLet’s express a from the first equation: a=10 -4c.\n\nSubstitute into the second equation:\n\n(10 -4c)^2 +16c(10 -4c) +64c^2=135\n\nCompute:\n\n(100 -80c +16c2) +160c -64c2 +64c2\n\n=